WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:07.290
Bonnie's burial was attended only by close friends&nbsp;
and relatives numbering about 150 including her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:11.910
sister, Billie Mace, who was a prisoner of the&nbsp;
law charged with the killing of two state highway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:17.100
patrolmen for which time her sister Bonnie had&nbsp;
been accused. Billy Mace was attended at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.870
funeral by two deputy sheriff's and a matron of&nbsp;
the jail. Clyde Barrow's mother and father seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:27.120
in the background and his brother also paid their&nbsp;
respects to the girl who was Clyde's only friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.390
and who died by his side. In the last few words of&nbsp;
Bonnie's own poem, which she penned just prior to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:41.190
her death: "To a few it means grief, to the law&nbsp;
its relief, but it's death to Bonnie and Clyde."

